ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:35:# Intern guide: specializing Candela on its scene data
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:37:This guide is for the person who will make the compiler specialize Candela, the gallery's path tracer, on the data that describes a scene. It explains every part of the system that work touches, the theory it rests on, a measurement made for this guide that says how much there is to gain, a design, and a phased plan in which every phase ends with a number.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:41:- **Paths are relative to the repository root.** A number after a colon is a line number at commit `a4beda6`, the commit this guide was written against. The ticket directory, `ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/`, is abbreviated `IRWB-007/`.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:49:Specializing an interpreter on a fixed program yields a compiled version of that program. This is the **first Futamura projection** (Futamura, 1971), and it is what this ticket proposes: specialize Candela on a scene's buffers, and the result is that scene, compiled.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:51:What exists today is a narrow case of it. The per-scene kernels of IRWB-001 specialize on one integer, `K.scene`, which selects one of 18 hand-generated scene sections. That cut the image by 58% but instructions per pixel by only 5–6% (IRWB-001 step 25), because the dispatch it removed was cheap. Everything the kernel reads from the buffers is still read, per ray, at run time.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:57:| operations a pixel executes | 3,910 | `various/03-binding-time-profile-all.json` |
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:60:| residual after ideal **polyvariant** specialization (live dynamic work) | 1,285 | same |
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:67:The hypothesis the user proposed, per-pixel gains of multiples rather than percents, is therefore possible on this program, but only in part of the gallery and only for a specializer that makes several versions of a function or a loop when the static data differs (a **polyvariant** specializer). It can hold for the scenes whose cost is the interpretation of the object table and the BVH. It cannot hold for the scenes whose cost is arithmetic on the ray, whatever the specializer. A specializer that gives every function one binding-time classification gets percents, because the object index, the BVH node index and the light index reach the readers as dynamic values. The design follows from that:
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:71:3. **A specializer built in four phases:** constant-address loads (U); calls and loops with static arguments (objects, leaves); the BVH walk as polyvariant program points; and splitting on values a dynamic test selects (which object a ray hit). Each phase is measured against the bound above and against IRWB-003's B-01 result of 8,267.7 instructions per pixel.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:83:- **The scene sections** (`scenes.typed`, 177 functions named `s<k>_…`): for each of the 18 scenes, its materials (`s0_mat_0`, `s0_mat_1`, …), its user distance fields, height fields, densities and custom integrator, as Typed code with the scene's constants written in. Candela's generator produced them from each scene's material graph, so they are **staged by hand**: the generator did, for materials, exactly the specialization this ticket wants the compiler to do for the rest.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:202:These sizes bound the code a specializer can create. Unrolling every loop over objects and every BVH walk in the largest scene makes at most 24 copies of an intersection test and 16 states of a walk. The analysis in §16 relies on that.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:266:The BVH loop's own control is static: `sp` starts at 1 and changes by constants, and the node indices it pushes come from BV rows. But it runs under a dynamic test, `hitAABB`, that depends on the ray. Whether a specializer can keep `sp` static there is the central question of §16.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:272:## 5. Specialization today
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:274:`src/typed/specialize.ts` (143 lines) is the project's whole specializer. Read it before anything else in this ticket; the design extends each of its pieces.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:276:**The fact.** A specialization fact is an artifact of the IR `typed.bindings` (`BindingsIR`, `specialize.ts:49–65`):
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:279:export interface Binding {                // specialize.ts:27–41
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:289:The artifact's revision hash makes the fact citable: `factRef(art, b)` (`:68`) is `fact:typed.bindings:<id>@<revision prefix>#<entity>`, and every edit made on the strength of the fact carries it as its `because`. `Program.why` on anything the specialization removed reaches the scene.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:291:**The pass.** `specialize` (`:71–143`) is an ordinary `typedPass` that writes only values. For the field form it reads the record layout from the front end (`st.frontEnd.records.K.scene.offset` is 28), finds every `load32[get p + 28]` where `p` is a K parameter whose only reaching definition is itself, and replaces it by the constant. Before rewriting, it refuses (`TxError('unsupported')`) when the function stores to that field through such a parameter (`:99–109`): a static check that the fact cannot be falsified inside the function. The ordinary passes then do the rest: `constprop`, `fold` and `cfg` resolve the dispatchers' comparisons and branches, and `dce` removes the dead arms.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:293:**The assumption.** Specializing changes what the program means for inputs that violate the fact, so the contract changes with it. `Program.specialize(bindings)` (`src/typed/program.ts:186–196`) first calls `Program.assume` (`:166–184`), which records, for every function with a K parameter `q_k`, the assumption `q_k.scene = v`. The `inputs` getter (`:142–161`) turns a dotted assumption into the case specification `{ record: { scene: { oneOf: [v] } } }` inside that parameter's spec, so the meaning check compares the program with r0 only on inputs where the field holds the value. Each revision records the assumptions it was made under (`Revision.assumptions`, `:63–64`).
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:297:**The per-scene pipeline and what it bought.** IRWB-001's `scripts/12-candela-per-scene.mjs` opens Candela with the four entries and `candelaInputs`, applies `specialize` with `{ record: 'K', field: 'scene', value, entity: 'scene/<id>' }`, runs `constprop, fold, cfg, dce, cfg`, then `dfe`:
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:325:**The frame comparison** is the check that ties a specialized program to the general one on real data. IRWB-001's `scripts/11-candela-render-compare.mjs`, `12-…` and `13-…`, and IRWB-003's `scripts/08-candela-scene-speed.mjs`, render the scene with the specialized program (evaluator or our image on the emulator) and with the reference compiler's `kernel` image on the emulator, and compare every output float bit for bit. At stride 8 it covers 400 pixels of a 160 × 160 scene and 100 of an 80 × 80 one, one sample each; that is 1.6% of the pixels. The design of §13 keeps it as the decisive check and adds a stronger sampled check on real rows.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:329:## 7. What a specializer can build on
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:340:**Inlining** (IRWB-003, unmerged). A call inlined at its site is the simplest form of a specialized version: one copy of the callee per call site, exposed to the caller's constants. `oRow` (7 statements) and `lRow` (7) are below IRWB-003's default size limit of 16 statements (its diary, step 8), so after inlining, a call `oRow(q_k, 0, 1)` with constant arguments becomes four loads at constant positions, which Phase 1's rule folds.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:346:A **partial evaluator** takes a program and some of its inputs, and produces a **residual program** that, given the remaining inputs, computes what the original would have computed on all of them. The inputs given in advance are **static**; the others are **dynamic**. Each value and operation of the program then has a **binding time**: static if it can be computed from static inputs alone, dynamic otherwise. The partial evaluator computes the static operations and emits code only for the dynamic ones. The standard reference is Jones, Gomard and Sestoft's book (1993); the terms below are theirs.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:357:The **first Futamura projection** states that specializing an interpreter on a program yields a compiled program (Futamura, 1971). The two further projections, specializing the specializer on the interpreter (a compiler) and on itself (a compiler generator), are not needed here. What matters is the first: the kernel interprets the scene buffers, so the residual is the scene compiled. The quality of the result depends on how much of the interpretation the specializer can make static, and that is what the measurement in §9 estimates.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:361:- **Offline and online.** An *offline* partial evaluator first runs a **binding-time analysis** (BTA) that labels every operation static or dynamic from the binding times of the inputs, then specializes by following the labels. An *online* one decides during specialization, looking at the actual static values. Offline is predictable and fast; online can exploit a value that happens to be static on one path but not another. IRWB-006's SCCP is online in this sense: it folds what turns out to be constant on the executable edges.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:362:- **Monovariant and polyvariant.** A *monovariant* analysis gives each function (or program point) one binding-time signature. A *polyvariant* specializer makes several versions of a function or a program point, one per distinct combination of static values that reaches it. `oRow` called with object 0 and with object 1 needs two versions to fold both; a monovariant analysis must call its index argument dynamic, because one of the calls has a dynamic index.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:363:- **Unfolding and memoization.** Unfolding a call replaces it by the specialized body (inlining is unfolding). Memoization keeps a table from (function, static arguments) to a residual function, so that equal static arguments share one version. It also makes recursion and loops terminate.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:364:- **Program-point specialization and generalization.** A loop head reached with different static states (`sp = 1`, then `sp = 2`, …) can be specialized into one residual block per state. If the set of states can grow without bound, the specializer must **generalize**: give up on some static values at that point (make them dynamic) so that the states repeat. Choosing what to generalize is the termination problem.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:366:- **Code growth.** Every version, unrolled iteration and case of The Trick is code. A specializer that never generalizes may not terminate; one that never splits gains nothing. Budgets decide.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:370:Before designing anything, this guide measured how much of Candela's per-pixel work a specializer could remove at best. `scripts/03-binding-time-profile.mjs` does it in three steps.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:373:2. **Execute each pixel with a shadow interpreter.** For each pixel at stride 8, it executes `cdlRenderSpan(K, out, y, x, x + 1, 0)` with a copy of the evaluator's loop that also carries a **binding-time tag** for every local, every result leaf and every byte of memory. The tags are ordered C < K < M < X < D:
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:381:3. **Model three specializers.**
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:382:   - **data (polyvariant):** tags follow data only. A value assigned under a dynamic test stays static if the assigned value is. This is what a specializer sees that splits the continuation of every dynamic test and makes a version for every static state: an upper bound on what any partial evaluator that keeps the arithmetic can make static.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:383:   - **gen (polyvariant within a function):** as `data`, but a static value that a dynamic test selected (the object a hit test chose, say) becomes dynamic when it is returned from a function. The specializer may split continuations inside a function body but merges them at its return.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:395:**B-01 in detail** (`various/03-binding-time-profile-all.json`, 400 pixels):
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:412:And in the control mode: the object index reaches `oRow` as a dynamic value inside the BVH walk, so the O loads become X (239.1 per pixel) and the work around them D. What stays static is what does not depend on any index: U at constant positions (73.1 loads per pixel) and K's words. That is the monovariant specializer's 1.1–1.2×.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:441:- **Two kinds of scene.** In the first twelve scenes but X-04, most of a pixel's work reads the scene: the M share is 44–67% and `oRow` alone is 25–47%. There the polyvariant bound is 2.1–4.5×. In the ray-marched and volume scenes, a pixel executes 30,000 to 415,000 operations, almost all dynamic: distance-field and noise evaluation at every step of a march, density along the ray. Their M share is 1–13%, and no specializer that keeps the arithmetic gains more than 1.3×. X-04, the Menger sponge, is between the two: a distance field, but with a quarter of its work in the scene's tables.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:444:- **The high end is not free.** Where the bound's two ends differ much (X-03 1.04–1.30, G-03 1.01–1.19), the difference is C operations: loop counters and constant arithmetic in the noise and fbm loops, which O2 does not remove because it does not unroll. They count only if the specializer unrolls those loops.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:448:### 10.1 Binding-time analysis over memory
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:462:A polyvariant specializer instead specializes the loop head per static state. For a tree, the state when a node is popped is a function of that node alone: the stack below it holds the pending left siblings on its path. The number of states is therefore at most the number of nodes plus one (the empty stack). R-01's BVH, decoded from its rows:
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:474:The walk terminates because each node is pushed at most once (a tree has no cycles), and the specializer terminates because the memo table holds at most one entry per state and there are finitely many states. In the gallery, 16 states is the most any scene needs (R-05, 15 nodes). The guard `if (sp < 38)` is static in every state.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:476:Generalization still matters elsewhere. `tracePath`'s bounce loop runs up to `maxDepth + 1` times (6 for B-01) and carries state that dynamic events select: the current medium, whether the last bounce was specular, whether the AOV outputs are done. Specializing each iteration separately multiplies the loop body (934 statements at r0) by the trip count. The data mode's bound includes that; a practical specializer generalizes at the bounce loop's head.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:484:- **Values selected by dynamic tests:** `r.obj` after `traceAll`. In the data mode, `finishHit(q_k, r.obj, …)` is specialized per object, because every continuation is split. In the gen mode `r.obj` becomes dynamic at `traceAll`'s return, and the loads in `finishHit` become X (B-01: X rises from 38.3 to 126.1 per pixel). The Trick on `r.obj` (§17) recovers them for `nObj + 1` versions of the continuation.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:490:The bound counts operations, not instructions, and it assumes the specializer keeps the arithmetic. Three effects lie outside it:
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:516:                  ▼                        │  specialize K.scene       │
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:528:The pieces, in order of dependency: a fact artifact that describes the constant memory (§12), a promise that makes the fact an assumption of the contract and is checked (§13), and four specialization phases that use it (§14–§17), under a budget (§18), each keeping provenance (§19).
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:563:**How it is made.** A builder (Proposed `memoryFacts(program, scene)`) runs the static prefix of the host protocol on the evaluator at r0 (`CandelaHost` with `evaluate`) and reads the regions back through K's pointer words. This is exactly what measurement 3 does at its start (`scripts/03-binding-time-profile.mjs`, the `regions` array). The scene buffers are `kind: 'data'` with entities from the scene table's rows: object `i` is words `24i … 24i + 23` of O, light `j` words `24j …` of LI, node `n` words `8n … 8n + 7` of BV. The GGX table is computed by `cdlInit` and the sky table by `cdlSky`, so they are `kind: 'computed'` with the function and the revision. Each region lists the entries during which it holds: the five scene buffers and the GGX table are fixed before `cdlSky` and stay fixed through every `cdlRenderSpan`; the sky table holds only during `cdlRenderSpan`, because `cdlSky` writes it.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:567:**Why access paths, not addresses.** The host places buffers where it likes; `CandelaHost` puts the rows after the output buffer, and the device's host may not. The kernel never computes a buffer's address; it only loads it from K. A region named by its path is independent of the layout. The fold rule (§14) recognizes the path the same way `specialize` recognizes `K.scene`.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:577:1. **Static: no store reaches a promised region.** For every function reachable from the entries, classify every `store` by its address, as measurement 2 does: through a checked address on a local holding a K buffer, into a K field through a K parameter, into a frame array, or unclassified. Refuse the specialization (`TxError('unsupported')`, as `specialize.ts:99–109` does for a field) if a store targets a promised region or if any store is unclassified. At r0 the check passes: `cdlRenderSpan` reaches stores into the output buffer, K's scratch fields and frame arrays only, and `cdlSky` writes only the sky table, which is not promised during its calls. A store into the output buffer is safe because the promise says the output buffer overlaps no region; the static check cannot show that, so the sampled check's cases lay the buffers out disjointly (the evaluator's `Memory.alloc` does by construction, `evaluate.ts:66`) and the runtime guard, if used, compares addresses too. The check must be re-run on every revision the specializer works on, because a later pass could in principle introduce a store; it is cheap (one walk over statements).
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:579:3. **The frame comparison** stays the decisive check. It runs the whole protocol on the specialized program and compares every output float with the reference image's, bit for bit.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:581:**An optional runtime guard** (Proposed variant). The promise can also be checked by the residual program itself. A guard at the entry compares the regions with the promised words and calls the general kernel on any difference. With the guard, the specialized image means what the general one means on every input, and no contract restriction is needed (this is IRWB-002's item E2, guarded specialization, applied to memory). Its cost per call is a comparison of B-01's 248 scene words. The sky table's 12,288 words are better covered by comparing the U rows `cdlSky` reads, since `cdlSky` is deterministic. Spread over a span of 160 pixels at about 8,000 instructions each, that is far below 1%; for one-pixel spans it is not. Whether the guard is worth its code is a decision for the device integration (§25).
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:609:The rewrite then replaces each folded `load` by its constant; the folded `caddr` under it disappears with it. A `ptr` value is never materialized: `get q_bO` stays where it is not folded away, and `dce` removes the assignment once nothing reads it. Every replacement carries `because: [factRef(memoryFacts, region entity of word w)]`. After it, `fold`, `cfg` and `dce` do what they do after `specialize`: `nNodes > 0`, `spp`, `mode === 20`, `q_k.spectral` and the sun and fog flags become constant branches, and their dead arms go.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:624:specializeCall(call g(args) in f):                    # Proposed: a program pass 'clone'
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:644:The leaf loop over `k < cntN` and the leaf's object indices `a.w + k` become static only if the node index `ni` is static, which requires the walk's state `(sp, stack[0 … sp))` to stay static under `hitAABB`. The phase specializes the walk by executing its static part at compile time and emitting a residual block for each static state.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:647:specializeWalk(traceAll, facts):                       # Proposed, on one version of traceAll
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:738:## 20. Hand staging against automatic specialization
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:740:The gallery's generator already specialized Candela by hand for part of each scene: the materials, user distance fields, height fields, densities and custom integrators are emitted per scene as the `s<k>_` sections, with constants written in (§1). The rest stays in buffers. The same program therefore contains both approaches side by side, which allows a comparison that is rarely possible: hand staging and automatic specialization on one program, judged by one measure.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:757:1. **Measure the two parts separately** after each phase, with the profile of §9 on the specialized revision: operations, static and residual, in `s<k>_` functions and in kernel functions. Automatic specialization has matched the hand staging when the kernel's remaining work is as little static as the sections' is: at r0 on B-01, 2.8 of the sections' 26.3 operations per pixel are M (11%), against 1,999 of the kernel's 3,884.2 (51%).
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:758:2. **Undo the staging for one material.** Write a small Typed interpreter for one material kind (a lobe chosen by a table row of parameters instead of literals), give scene 0's two materials as rows in a new region, and specialize. If the specializer reproduces `s0_mat_0`'s code (the same residual operations per call, the same constants), it has matched the generator on its own ground. This needs no Candela tooling, only a corpus project, and it is the cleanest test of the claim that the compiler can do the generator's job.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:759:3. **Report the division.** Compare the generator's binding-time division (what it put in code, what in buffers) with the specializer's (what its BTA or its online run found static). A place where the specializer finds static work inside a section is something the generator left on the table.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:765:| D1 | regions are named by access path from K (`K.bO`), not by address | absolute address ranges | layout-independent; the kernel only reaches buffers through K; matches how `specialize` recognizes `K.scene` | proposed |
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:767:| D3 | an online specializer (SCCP extended with memory, then versions) rather than an offline BTA first | offline BTA with annotations | measurement 3: a monovariant division is worth at most 1.68× on any scene (1.12–1.21× on B-01), the polyvariant one up to 2.66–3.04× on B-01 and 4.51–4.94× on R-01 | proposed |
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:770:| D6 | the static prefix (`cdlLayout`, `cdlInit`, `bind`, `cdlSky`) runs on the evaluator at compile time to produce the fact | recompute the tables in the specializer by partial evaluation of `cdlSky` | the evaluator is the specification; the tables are computed facts with provenance; `cdlSky` runs once per bind and is not hot | proposed |
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:779:- **The promise can be broken by the host.** On the device, the host binds a scene and renders; if it ever rebinds without recompiling, the specialized image computes the old scene. The runtime guard (§13) turns that into a fallback instead of a wrong frame. Without it, the per-scene image must be keyed by the fact's revision hash.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:786:**Hypothesis.** Specializing Candela on a scene's constant memory reduces instructions per pixel by a factor, not a percentage. Concretely: on B-01 and R-01, the per-scene program after this ticket's phases executes at most half the instructions per pixel of IRWB-003's per-scene results (8,267.7 and 18,186.7), with frames bit-identical to the reference.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:790:- **Monovariant specialization is excluded.** Its bound is at most 1.68× on any gallery scene (L-03) and 1.12–1.21× on B-01. No design that keeps one version per function can reach a factor of 2.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:791:- **The ray-marched and volume scenes are excluded.** On X-04, V-03, V-04, R-08, X-03, R-04 and G-03 even the polyvariant bound is below 1.7×. Their cost is dynamic float arithmetic, which no specializer that keeps the arithmetic removes. For them the hypothesis is false; their gains, if any, come from elsewhere (IRWB-003's code generation, inlining, float facts).
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:805:**Phase 0: the measuring harness.** Move `scripts/03-binding-time-profile.mjs` into `tools/` as a profile any revision can be given (it takes the store to execute). Add the per-scene instruction measurement for a saved head, which IRWB-003's `scripts/08-candela-scene-speed.mjs` already does. *Acceptance:* the profile reproduces measurement 3's B-01 numbers exactly on r0; on the per-scene revision after `specialize` and `dfe` it reports 0 K operations and 0 differing floats.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:811:**Phase 3: the BVH walk.** Program-point specialization of `traceAll`'s walk with the stack as per-state values, and generalization when the states exceed the budget. *Acceptance:* in every gallery scene, the walk is replaced by a tree of box tests with as many blocks as the walk has states (B-01: 2 = 1 node + 1; R-01: 8); BV loads per pixel 0; frames bit-identical; instructions per pixel. Falsifier 1 of §23 is checked here.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-007--specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory/design-doc/01-intern-guide-specializing-candela-on-its-scene-data-partial-evaluation-over-constant-memory.md:827:1. **The device's code limit, and where per-scene images are compiled.** The gallery compiles its Typed module on the device on first use after a firmware update, in about 85 s, and caches it (`app.json` notes). Eighteen specialized images either multiply that work or must be compiled ahead and shipped; the cache must then be keyed by the fact's revision hash. The limit on code size decides the budget of §18 (decision D7).
